  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 PM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nModels are in good agreement with each other and with satellite \nimagery showing the low pressure system moving inland into British \nColumbia and a deep trough pushing into the Pacific Northwest. We \nalready see the high clouds spilling over the ridge as it breaks \ndown ahead of the trough. Look for the trough to move in over the \nRockies by Friday evening with a couple embedded shortwave systems \npassing Friday evening and the stronger Saturday morning.  This \nsystem quickly moves out onto the Plains Saturday evening. Models \nindicate ridging building in Sunday, but getting knocked down Monday \nby another shortwave system. A deeper, more southern system moves in \nover the Desert Southwest through mid next week to possibly bring a \nreturn of moisture to the southern mountains, but this far out, \nconfidence is low. \n\nExpect fair, dry weather today into Friday with high clouds and \nlight winds across the region. Clouds will move in from the \nnorthwest Friday with a few light snow showers across the northern \nColorado mountains through the afternoon. Snow increases over the \nPark and Elkhead mountains Friday evening, spreading south into the \nElk and West Elk mountains by Saturday morning. The higher peaks of \nthe Park Range may see a foot of new snow, but most of the area will \nsee lesser amounts of 6-12 inches. Issued a Winter Weather Advisory \nfor the Park and Elkhead mountains for six to 12 inches snow with \nlocally higher amounts possibly on the higher peaks between 5 PM \nFriday and 5 AM Sunday. Farther south across the Flat Tops and the \nCentral Colorado mountains, look for four to eight inches snow \naccumulation by Sunday morning. Issued a Winter Weather Advisory for \nthese areas for up to eight inches new snow during the same period. \n\nDrier weather returns Sunday, but expect lingering light showers \npossible in the mountains. The shortwave Monday brings more showers \nto the northern mountains and Flat Tops with a chance in the central \nColorado mountains, but it's too soon to tell how much snow and what \nimpacts will come with this system. Stay tuned for updates on this. \nThen the midweek low moving in over the Southwest will bring \nwidespread showers across the higher terrain of eastern Utah and \nWestern Colorado. This one is also too soon to know how much \nmoisture will accompany it, but it could be a significant storm with \na subtropical fetch of moisture. Stay tuned for updates on this one \ntoo.",
